  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it in a browser here, so none of the visuals, shaders or animation have been seen on screen. The `vite build` step wasn't run either.\n\n- **Island:** one flat-shaded custom mesh built in code. It has a jittered polar terrain grid with a hill and per-face colours (sand, grass, rock, pale peak), plus pine trees and rocks. The trees and rocks are placed from a seeded random generator and use the same height function as the terrain.\n- **Water:** a large wave surface with a custom shader. Waves are animated through a `waveClock` uniform, with Fresnel reflection of the sky, sun/moon glints, crest highlights and a fade into the horizon colour. It is translucent, so the submerged shore shows through.\n- **Sky and cycle:** a sky dome with its own shader (gradient, sun and moon discs, stars at night). A full day/night cycle takes 20 seconds. Each frame the sun, moon and a hemisphere fill light change position, intensity and colour. The sky and water shaders share the same colour uniforms.\n- **Never black:** the lighting always has a hemisphere fill plus a low neutral environment light, so the scene stays lit at night.\n- **Camera:** it sits just above the water, with the island in the lower middle of the frame and some sky above. For the slow orbit I rotate the island under the fixed camera instead of moving the camera, because I wasn't sure `app.camera.setPose` would take effect.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()`.\n\nI didn't tune shadow bias on the faceted terrain, so shadow acne is possible. The exact brightness of the custom-shader colours also hasn't been checked, since I don't know what tone mapping the engine applies to them.",
